`enter(number, args...)` is the instruction that enters the kernel. On Linux x86-64, aarch64
and riscv64 it is inline assembly with one overload per argument count, 0 to 6, each binding
exactly the registers it uses through register variables, so the compiler loads the arguments in
place and keeps everything else live across the call. Memory is clobbered, since the kernel reads
and writes through the pointers it is given. It returns the raw kernel value: -errno, between
-4095 and -1, on failure; `invoke` turns that into a `ca::result`.

`read_own(address, out)` copies the process's own memory through `process_vm_readv`, which
fails instead of faulting on an unmapped address: the backtrace walks frame records with it, and
`elf::image::program()` reads the ELF header of a static PIE. It goes through `enter`, so a
failure leaves the last error as it was.

`inline_entry` tells whether `enter` is the inlined instruction (true on those three CPUs) or a
call to libc's `syscall(2)`. Functions that must not return, such as `exit`, are generated as
`[[noreturn]]` and end in `ca::unreachable()` after the instruction.
